   Note that tracking each dropped target requires up to 10Kb of RAM. Therefore, big values for `-promscrape.maxDroppedTargets`
   may result in increased memory usage if a large number of scrape targets are dropped during relabeling.
 
+* If a misconfigured service discovery source (a broadened catalog query, a removed tag filter, etc.) suddenly returns
+  a huge number of targets for a single `job_name`, this can spike `vmagent` memory and CPU usage and degrade scraping
+  for other jobs. To protect against this, set the `max_scrape_targets` option for the affected `job_name` (or the
+  `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargets` command-line flag for a global default) — see
+  [scrape_config enhancements](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs). By default
+  there is no limit. When the limit is exceeded, `vmagent` keeps scraping only the first `max_scrape_targets` targets for
+  the job, while the excluded ones show up as dropped targets (reason "too many targets") at `http://vmagent-host:8429/targets`,
+  `http://vmagent-host:8429/service-discovery` and `http://vmagent-host:8429/api/v1/targets`, so it is easy to spot which
+  job(s) are currently over the limit and how many targets are being excluded.
+
 * It is recommended to increase `-remoteWrite.queues` if `vmagent_remotewrite_pending_data_bytes` [metric](#monitoring)
   grows constantly. It is also recommended to increase `-remoteWrite.maxBlockSize` and `-remoteWrite.maxRowsPerBlock` command-line flags in this case.
   This can improve data ingestion performance to the configured remote storage systems at the cost of higher memory usage.
